  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prior: P(Disease) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(No Disease) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|Disease) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|No Disease) = 0.95 → P(+|No Disease) = 0.05\n\n**Calculation:**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)P(D) + P(+|¬D)P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.04990 = 0.05186\n\nP(Disease | +) = P(+|D)P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\n**Result:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nSo even with a positive test, there's only about a 3.78% chance the person actually has the condition — a consequence of the condition's rarity combined with the test's imperfect specificity (the 5% false-positive rate, applied to the huge \"no disease\" group, generates far more false positives than true positives from the tiny \"disease\" group).\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
